) values
  ('fin-001', 'The 1.4% fee eating your mutual fund', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'What is the main difference between a direct and a regular mutual fund plan?', array['Direct plans have a lower expense ratio (no distributor commission)', 'Direct plans invest in different stocks', 'Regular plans are managed by better fund managers', 'Direct plans are only for large investors'], 0, 'You are paying a 1.4% fee on your mutual fund that costs you ₹14 lakh over 20 years. Here is what to do instead.', 'Every mutual fund charges an expense ratio — a yearly fee taken from your money whether the fund gains or loses. Regular plans pay a distributor commission; direct plans of the same fund skip it.', 'A ₹10,000/month SIP for 20 years at 12% grows to about ₹99 lakh in a direct plan (0.5% fee) but only ~₹85 lakh in a regular plan (1.9% fee). Same fund, same manager — ₹14 lakh difference.', 'Same fund, direct plan: the word "Direct" in the name saves you lakhs.', 'Open your MF statement and check if your plans say "Direct" or "Regular".', 92, 'en'),
  ('fin-002', 'SIP: autopilot investing in 60 seconds', 'finance', 50, null, null, 'What does rupee-cost averaging in a SIP do?', array['Guarantees fixed returns every year', 'Buys more units when prices are low, fewer when high', 'Avoids all market risk', 'Doubles your money every 5 years'], 1, '₹500 a month can become ₹5 lakh. Not magic — just a SIP started early.', 'A Systematic Investment Plan (SIP) auto-invests a fixed amount into a mutual fund every month. It removes timing decisions and buys more units when markets fall (rupee-cost averaging).', 'Arjun starts a ₹2,000/month SIP in an index fund at 21. At 12% average returns, by 35 he has ~₹7.5 lakh — while investing only ₹3.4 lakh of his own money.', 'Start small, start monthly, start now — the date matters more than the amount.', 'Check the minimum SIP amount on any index fund — most start at ₹100–500.', 90, 'en'),
  ('fin-003', 'Emergency fund before everything else', 'finance', 40, null, null, 'Where should an emergency fund be kept?', array['In small-cap stocks for growth', 'In crypto for quick access', 'In instant-access savings or sweep-in FDs', 'Locked in a 5-year tax-saver FD'], 2, 'One hospital bill can wipe out two years of investing. Unless you build this first.', 'An emergency fund is 3–6 months of expenses kept in instant-access savings — not stocks. It exists so a job loss or medical bill never forces you to sell investments at a loss or take a personal loan at 14%.', 'Priya spends ₹40,000/month. Her target: ₹1.2–2.4 lakh parked in a sweep-in FD. When her laptop died mid-project, she paid cash instead of swiping a credit card at 42% annual interest.', '3–6 months of expenses in cash first — investing comes second.', 'Multiply your monthly spend by 3. That number is your first savings goal.', 88, 'en'),
  ('fin-004', 'Credit card interest: the 42% trap', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'Roughly what annual interest do credit cards charge on unpaid balances?', array['8–10%', '15–18%', '24–30%', '36–45%'], 3, 'The "minimum amount due" button is the most expensive button on your phone.', 'Credit cards charge ~3.5% per month — around 42% a year — on unpaid balances. Paying only the minimum due keeps the debt alive for years while interest compounds against you.', 'A ₹50,000 bill paid at 5% minimum due takes over 6 years to clear and costs ~₹48,000 in interest — nearly doubling the price of everything you bought.', 'Pay the full bill every month, or don’t swipe.', 'Turn on auto-pay for the TOTAL due, not the minimum due.', 91, 'en'),
  ('fin-005', 'Index funds: beat 80% of pros doing nothing', 'finance', 50, null, null, 'Why do index funds often beat actively managed funds long-term?', array['They take higher risks for higher returns', 'Their fees are far lower and they avoid stock-picking errors', 'They are guaranteed by the government', 'They only buy rising stocks'], 1, 'Most professional fund managers lose to a fund that has no manager at all.', 'An index fund simply buys every stock in an index like the Nifty 50 in the same proportion. No stock-picking, tiny fees (~0.2%), and over 10+ years it beats the majority of actively managed funds.', 'Over the last decade, a majority of large-cap active funds in India underperformed the Nifty 50 after fees. A ₹5,000/month SIP in a Nifty index fund quietly compounded without a single decision.', 'Owning the whole market cheaply beats guessing which stock wins.', 'Look up the expense ratio of one Nifty 50 index fund and one active large-cap fund.', 93, 'en'),
  ('fin-006', 'The 50-30-20 rule for your salary', 'finance', 40, null, null, 'In the 50-30-20 rule, what does the 20% represent?', array['Rent and utilities', 'Entertainment and shopping', 'Savings and investments', 'Taxes'], 2, 'Salary gone by the 20th? You don’t need to earn more — you need three buckets.', 'Split take-home pay into 50% needs (rent, food, transport), 30% wants (eating out, shopping), and 20% savings/investments. The 20% moves out on salary day — before you can spend it.', 'On a ₹60,000 take-home: ₹30,000 for needs, ₹18,000 for wants, ₹12,000 auto-moved to a SIP + emergency fund on the 1st. Spending adjusts to what’s left, not the other way around.', 'Save first, spend what remains — never the reverse.', 'Set up an auto-transfer of 20% of your salary for the day after payday.', 87, 'en'),
  ('tech-001', 'APIs explained with a restaurant', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'In the restaurant analogy, what role does the API play?', array['The kitchen that cooks the food', 'The waiter carrying requests and responses', 'The customer ordering food', 'The bill you pay at the end'], 1, 'Every app you use is secretly a customer at a restaurant. Here’s the menu.', 'An API (Application Programming Interface) is how software asks other software for things. Like a waiter: you (the app) order from a menu (the API), the kitchen (the server) prepares it, the waiter brings back exactly what you asked for.', 'When Zomato shows you "25 min delivery", it called Google Maps’ API with two locations, and the API returned the travel time. Zomato never built its own maps.', 'APIs let apps borrow each other’s superpowers instead of rebuilding them.', 'Notice one app feature today that’s clearly powered by another company’s API.', 94, 'en'),
  ('tech-002', 'Why your app is slow: the cache story', 'technology', 50, null, null, 'What does a cache fundamentally do?', array['Compresses data to save space', 'Encrypts data for security', 'Stores copies of data closer to the user for faster access', 'Deletes old data automatically'], 2, 'Instagram loads in 1 second. Your bank app takes 10. The difference is one idea.', 'A cache stores copies of data close to where it’s needed, so repeat requests skip the slow trip to the original source. Reading from memory is thousands of times faster than a database or network call.', 'Instagram caches your feed on your phone — reopening shows old posts instantly while new ones load behind the scenes. Railway booking sites cache seat availability for a few seconds to survive tatkal rush.', 'Fast apps don’t fetch faster — they avoid fetching at all.', 'Open any app in airplane mode and see what still loads. That’s the cache.', 90, 'en'),
  ('tech-003', 'Passwords are dead. Hashes run the world.', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'Why is hashing safer than storing passwords directly?', array['Hashes are one-way — the original password can’t be read back from them', 'Hashes are stored on a different server', 'Hashes change every day automatically', 'Hashes are shorter so they’re harder to steal'], 0, 'No serious website knows your password. Not even Google. Here’s the trick.', 'Sites store a hash — a one-way scrambled fingerprint of your password. When you log in, they hash what you typed and compare fingerprints. The original password is never saved, so a database leak doesn’t directly reveal it.', '"priya123" always hashes to the same gibberish like "ef92b7…". A hacker stealing the database gets fingerprints, not passwords — unless your password is common enough to be in a precomputed list. That’s why length beats cleverness.', 'Websites store fingerprints of passwords, not passwords — long ones stay safe.', 'Change one weak password to a 4-word phrase like "mango-train-cricket-lamp".', 89, 'en'),
  ('tech-004', 'What "the cloud" actually is', 'technology', 40, null, null, 'What is the core economic advantage of cloud computing?', array['Data is stored more securely than on your own servers', 'You pay only for capacity you actually use and can scale instantly', 'It works without internet', 'It makes apps automatically faster'], 1, 'Your photos aren’t floating in the sky. They’re in a building in Mumbai with very good AC.', 'The cloud is just other people’s computers — massive data centers you rent by the second. Instead of buying servers, companies pay AWS, Google, or Azure only for what they use, and scale up or down instantly.', 'Hotstar handles 25 million concurrent IPL viewers by renting thousands of extra cloud servers for match hours, then releasing them at night. Owning that hardware for 4 hours of daily peak would be ruinous.', 'The cloud means renting computing like electricity — pay for what you use.', 'Check where your phone backs up photos — that’s a data center, not the sky.', 88, 'en'),
  ('tech-005', 'How AI models actually "learn"', 'technology', 55, null, null, 'At its core, what does a large language model do?', array['Searches the internet for answers in real time', 'Stores every conversation and replays the best one', 'Predicts the most likely next word based on learned patterns', 'Follows grammar rules written by linguists'], 2, 'ChatGPT has never understood a single word you typed. So how does it answer?', 'Large language models learn patterns from billions of sentences: given these words, which word most likely comes next? Training adjusts millions of internal dials until predictions match real text. Intelligence emerges from prediction, not comprehension.', 'Type "Mumbai is the financial capital of" — the model has seen this pattern so often that "India" gets the highest probability. Multiply that trick by trillions of patterns and you get essays, code, and poems.', 'AI predicts the next word so well that it looks like understanding.', 'Ask an AI the same question twice — slightly different answers reveal the probability machine.', 95, 'en'),
  ('tech-006', 'Frontend vs backend in one metro ride', 'technology', 40, null, null, 'Which of these is a backend responsibility?', array['Button animations', 'Processing the payment and storing the ticket', 'The color scheme of the app', 'The swipe gesture to go back'], 1, 'The metro station you see is 10% of the metro. The other 90% decides if your train arrives.', 'Frontend is everything the user sees and touches — screens, buttons, animations. Backend is the hidden machinery — databases, servers, payment processing, logic. They talk through APIs.', 'Booking a metro ticket on Paytm: the frontend shows the map and the pay button. The backend checks fares, talks to the bank, generates the QR code, and stores your ticket — all invisible.', 'Frontend is the station you see; backend is the network that runs the trains.', 'Next time an app shows a spinner, that’s the frontend waiting on the backend.', 86, 'en'),
  ('comm-001', 'The STAR method: answer anything in 60 seconds', 'communication', 50, null, null, 'What does the R in STAR stand for?', array['Reason', 'Response', 'Result', 'Reflection'], 2, '"Tell me about a time you failed" — the question that breaks interviews. Unless you know STAR.', 'STAR = Situation, Task, Action, Result. Describe the context in one line, your responsibility, exactly what YOU did, and end with a measurable outcome. It turns rambling into a story with a punchline.', 'Priya in her appraisal: "Our campaign was underperforming (S). I owned the relaunch (T). I A/B tested three creatives and moved budget to reels (A). CTR rose 40% in two weeks (R)." Ninety seconds, unforgettable.', 'Situation, Task, Action, Result — end every work story with a number.', 'Write one STAR story from your last month of work — you’ll reuse it for years.', 93, 'en'),
  ('comm-002', 'Silence: the negotiation weapon nobody uses', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'Why is silence effective after stating your position in a negotiation?', array['It shows you are angry', 'The discomfort pressures the other side to concede or improve the offer', 'It gives you time to check your phone', 'It signals you have accepted their terms'], 1, 'The person who speaks first after a salary offer usually loses. Here’s why.', 'Humans rush to fill silence, and in that rush they concede. After you state your number — or hear theirs — stop talking. The pressure of silence transfers to the other side, and they often improve the offer just to end it.', 'HR: "We can do ₹12 LPA." You: "I was expecting ₹15 based on my offers." Then silence. Ten awkward seconds later: "Let me check if ₹14 is possible." You said nothing and earned ₹2 lakh.', 'State your number, then stop talking — silence negotiates for you.', 'In your next disagreement, pause 5 full seconds before responding.', 91, 'en'),
  ('comm-003', 'Write emails people actually answer', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'Where should the main request go in a work email?', array['At the end, after full context', 'In the first line', 'In the subject only', 'In a follow-up email'], 1, 'Your 8-paragraph email was never read. The reply you wanted fits in 4 lines.', 'Busy people scan, they don’t read. Lead with the ask in line one, give only decision-critical context, end with a single clear question and a deadline. One email, one ask.', 'Instead of a long update ending in a buried request: "Hi Ravi — need your approval on the ₹80k Diwali ad budget by Friday. Context: last year’s ₹60k returned 3x. Approve, or want a call first?" Reply time: minutes, not days.', 'Ask first, context second, one question, one deadline.', 'Rewrite your next email so the request is the first sentence.', 89, 'en'),
  ('comm-004', 'Active listening: the promotion skill', 'communication', 50, null, null, 'What is the key behavior of active listening?', array['Nodding frequently while planning your reply', 'Taking detailed notes', 'Paraphrasing the speaker’s point back before responding', 'Maintaining constant eye contact'], 2, 'The best communicators in your office talk the least. They do this instead.', 'Active listening means proving you heard: paraphrase what they said ("So the concern is timeline, not budget?"), ask one clarifying question, and only then respond. People trust — and promote — those who make them feel understood.', 'A client rants about a delayed project. Instead of defending, Rahul says: "You committed a date to your boss based on ours, and we made you look bad — is that the core issue?" The client calms instantly. The relationship survives.', 'Paraphrase before you respond — being understood defuses almost anything.', 'In your next conversation, repeat back the other person’s point before making yours.', 90, 'en'),
  ('comm-005', 'Present without slides: the rule of three', 'communication', 40, null, null, 'Why does the rule of three work in presentations?', array['Three points fill exactly ten minutes', 'It matches how much an audience can hold in memory and retell', 'Slides look better with three bullets', 'It is required in corporate templates'], 1, 'Steve Jobs, TED speakers, and your best professor all use the same cheat code: three.', 'Human memory holds about three things from any talk. Structure everything as three points: "Three reasons we should do this." Audiences follow, remember, and can even repeat your argument to others — which is how ideas spread.', 'Pitching a college fest sponsor: "Three things: 8,000 students in footfall, 40 colleges reached on social, ₹2 lakh in direct sales last year." The sponsor repeats those exact numbers to their manager the same day.', 'Say three things. People can’t remember four.', 'Structure your next update or pitch as exactly three points.', 87, 'en'),
  ('comm-006', 'Say no without burning bridges', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'What are the three parts of a graceful no?', array['Apologize, explain at length, promise to do it next time', 'Appreciate, decline with a reason, offer an alternative', 'Ignore, delay, hope they forget', 'Accept, then cancel later'], 1, 'Every "quick favour" you accept is a silent no to your own work. Learn the polite no.', 'A good no has three parts: appreciate the ask, decline with a brief honest reason, and offer an alternative (a later date, another person, a smaller scope). You protect your time without damaging the relationship.', '"Thanks for thinking of me for the fest committee — I can’t commit this month, I have finals. I could review your sponsorship deck by Sunday though, if that helps?" No guilt, no resentment, relationship intact.', 'Appreciate, decline, offer an alternative — a no can strengthen trust.', 'Decline one low-priority request today using the three-part structure.', 88, 'en'),
  ('prod-001', 'The 2-minute rule kills procrastination', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'How does the 2-minute rule apply to large tasks?', array['Skip them until you have 2 free hours', 'Split them among teammates', 'Shrink the starting action to something 2 minutes long', 'Set a 2-minute timer and work until it rings'], 2, 'You’ve rescheduled that task 6 times. It takes 90 seconds. Here’s the fix.', 'If something takes under 2 minutes, do it immediately — deciding, remembering, and re-reading it later costs more than doing it now. For big tasks, shrink the start to 2 minutes: "open the file" instead of "finish the report".', 'Reply to the professor’s email: 2 minutes — do it now. Write the assignment: shrink it to "open the doc and write one ugly sentence". Starting is the whole battle; momentum does the rest.', 'Under 2 minutes? Do it now. Too big? Shrink the start to 2 minutes.', 'Clear three 2-minute tasks right after this lesson.', 92, 'en'),
  ('prod-002', 'Deep work: 90 minutes beats 8 hours', 'productivity', 50, null, null, 'Roughly how long does it take to refocus after an interruption?', array['30 seconds', '5 minutes', '23 minutes', '2 hours'], 2, 'You worked 8 hours yesterday and produced almost nothing. This is why.', 'Every notification costs ~23 minutes of refocus time. Deep work means one 90-minute block: phone in another room, one task, one tab. Ninety focused minutes routinely out-produce a whole distracted day.', 'Rahul’s client work was taking 3 scattered days per design. He switched to two 90-minute morning blocks with his phone in the kitchen. Same designs now finish in one day — afternoons are free.', 'One 90-minute block, phone in another room — that IS the workday.', 'Block 90 minutes tomorrow morning; leave your phone physically elsewhere.', 91, 'en'),
  ('prod-003', 'Eat the frog: order beats effort', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'Why should the hardest task come first in the day?', array['Mornings are quieter in the office', 'Willpower and focus are highest early, before decisions drain them', 'It impresses your manager', 'Easy tasks are better after lunch'], 1, 'Your to-do list isn’t too long. It’s in the wrong order.', '"Eat the frog" means doing the hardest, most-avoided task first thing, when willpower is at its daily peak. Everything after feels easy, and the dread that drains you all day disappears by 10am.', 'Arjun kept "study accounts chapter" at the bottom of his list under easy errands. Flipped: accounts first at 8am, errands after. The chapter that haunted him for a week was done in 3 days.', 'Hardest task first — willpower is highest before the day erodes it.', 'Pick tomorrow’s frog tonight and write it on top of your list.', 88, 'en'),
  ('prod-004', 'Time-blocking: your calendar is the to-do list', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'What is the core idea of time-blocking?', array['Working in 25-minute sprints', 'Assigning tasks to specific calendar slots instead of keeping a list', 'Blocking distracting websites', 'Batching all meetings on one day'], 1, 'A to-do list says what. It never says when. That’s why yours never finishes.', 'Time-blocking assigns every task an actual slot on your calendar. A task with a time and duration gets done; a task on a list gets postponed. Unscheduled means unreal.', 'Priya’s "prepare presentation" sat on her list for 2 weeks. She blocked Tue 8–9:30am and Thu 8–9am on her calendar like meetings with herself. Done by Thursday — because it had an appointment.', 'Don’t list tasks — give them appointments.', 'Move your top 3 tasks from your list onto tomorrow’s calendar with times.', 89, 'en'),
  ('prod-005', 'The Pomodoro technique, minus the hype', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'Why does the 25-minute Pomodoro length work psychologically?', array['It matches the human attention span exactly', 'The small commitment lowers the resistance to starting', 'Breaks make work more fun', 'It fits neatly into an hour'], 1, 'Can’t focus for 2 hours? Good news: you only ever need to focus for 25 minutes.', 'Pomodoro = 25 minutes of single-task focus, then a 5-minute break, repeated. The short commitment defeats the resistance to starting, and the timer externalizes discipline so you don’t negotiate with yourself.', 'Facing a 40-page reading, Arjun commits to "one pomodoro, then I can stop". He finishes four back-to-back — because starting was the only hard part.', 'Commit to 25 minutes, not to finishing — starting is the real barrier.', 'Run one 25-minute timer on your most avoided task today.', 87, 'en'),
  ('fin-001-hi', 'आपके म्यूचुअल फंड की 1.4% फीस', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'डायरेक्ट और रेगुलर म्यूचुअल फंड प्लान में मुख्य अंतर क्या है?', array['डायरेक्ट प्लान का एक्सपेंस रेशियो कम होता है (कोई कमीशन नहीं)', 'डायरेक्ट प्लान अलग शेयरों में निवेश करता है', 'रेगुलर प्लान के फंड मैनेजर बेहतर होते हैं', 'डायरेक्ट प्लान सिर्फ बड़े निवेशकों के लिए है'], 0, 'आप अपने म्यूचुअल फंड पर 1.4% फीस दे रहे हैं जो 20 साल में ₹14 लाख खा जाती है। जानिए क्या करें।', 'हर म्यूचुअल फंड एक्सपेंस रेशियो लेता है — आपके पैसे से कटने वाली सालाना फीस, चाहे फंड बढ़े या गिरे। रेगुलर प्लान में डिस्ट्रीब्यूटर कमीशन जुड़ता है; उसी फंड का डायरेक्ट प्लान इससे बचाता है।', '₹10,000/माह की SIP, 20 साल, 12% रिटर्न: डायरेक्ट प्लान (0.5% फीस) में ~₹99 लाख, रेगुलर प्लान (1.9% फीस) में सिर्फ ~₹85 लाख। वही फंड, वही मैनेजर — ₹14 लाख का फर्क।', 'वही फंड, डायरेक्ट प्लान: नाम में "Direct" शब्द आपके लाखों बचाता है।', 'अपना MF स्टेटमेंट खोलें और देखें कि प्लान "Direct" है या "Regular"।', 92, 'hi'),
  ('fin-003-hi', 'सबसे पहले इमरजेंसी फंड', 'finance', 40, null, null, 'इमरजेंसी फंड कहाँ रखना चाहिए?', array['ग्रोथ के लिए स्मॉल-कैप शेयरों में', 'तुरंत पहुंच के लिए क्रिप्टो में', 'तुरंत निकलने वाली बचत या स्वीप-इन FD में', '5 साल की टैक्स-सेवर FD में लॉक करके'], 2, 'एक अस्पताल का बिल आपकी दो साल की बचत मिटा सकता है। जब तक आप पहले यह नहीं बनाते।', 'इमरजेंसी फंड यानी 3–6 महीने के खर्च जितनी रकम, तुरंत निकलने वाली बचत में — शेयरों में नहीं। ताकि नौकरी जाने या मेडिकल बिल पर आपको घाटे में निवेश बेचना या 14% पर पर्सनल लोन न लेना पड़े।', 'प्रिया का मासिक खर्च ₹40,000 है। उसका लक्ष्य: स्वीप-इन FD में ₹1.2–2.4 लाख। लैपटॉप खराब होने पर उसने 42% सालाना ब्याज वाले क्रेडिट कार्ड की जगह कैश से भुगतान किया।', 'पहले 3–6 महीने के खर्च कैश में — निवेश उसके बाद।', 'अपने मासिक खर्च को 3 से गुणा करें। वही आपका पहला बचत लक्ष्य है।', 88, 'hi'),
  ('tech-001-hi', 'API को रेस्टोरेंट से समझिए', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'रेस्टोरेंट की तुलना में API की भूमिका क्या है?', array['खाना बनाने वाली रसोई', 'ऑर्डर और जवाब लाने-ले जाने वाला वेटर', 'खाना ऑर्डर करने वाला ग्राहक', 'आखिर में आने वाला बिल'], 1, 'आपका हर ऐप चुपचाप किसी रेस्टोरेंट का ग्राहक है। यह रहा मेन्यू।', 'API यानी सॉफ्टवेयर का दूसरे सॉफ्टवेयर से कुछ मांगने का तरीका। वेटर की तरह: आप (ऐप) मेन्यू (API) से ऑर्डर करते हैं, रसोई (सर्वर) बनाती है, वेटर ठीक वही लाकर देता है जो आपने मांगा।', 'जब Zomato "25 मिनट डिलीवरी" दिखाता है, तो उसने Google Maps के API को दो लोकेशन भेजीं और API ने यात्रा का समय लौटाया। Zomato ने कभी अपने नक्शे नहीं बनाए।', 'API से ऐप्स एक-दूसरे की ताकत उधार लेते हैं, दोबारा नहीं बनाते।', 'आज किसी ऐप का एक फीचर पहचानिए जो साफ तौर पर किसी और कंपनी के API से चलता है।', 94, 'hi'),
  ('tech-005-hi', 'AI मॉडल असल में कैसे "सीखते" हैं', 'technology', 55, null, null, 'मूल रूप से लार्ज लैंग्वेज मॉडल क्या करता है?', array['रियल टाइम में इंटरनेट पर जवाब खोजता है', 'हर बातचीत सहेजकर सबसे अच्छी दोहराता है', 'सीखे पैटर्न से सबसे संभावित अगला शब्द भांपता है', 'भाषाविदों के लिखे व्याकरण नियमों पर चलता है'], 2, 'ChatGPT ने आपका लिखा एक शब्द भी कभी नहीं समझा। फिर जवाब कैसे देता है?', 'लार्ज लैंग्वेज मॉडल अरबों वाक्यों से पैटर्न सीखते हैं: इन शब्दों के बाद सबसे संभावित अगला शब्द क्या है? ट्रेनिंग लाखों अंदरूनी डायल तब तक घुमाती है जब तक अनुमान असली टेक्स्ट से मेल न खाए। समझ नहीं, अनुमान से बुद्धिमत्ता निकलती है।', 'लिखिए "मुंबई भारत की आर्थिक" — मॉडल ने यह पैटर्न इतनी बार देखा है कि "राजधानी" को सबसे ऊंची संभावना मिलती है। इसी तरकीब को खरबों पैटर्न से गुणा कीजिए — निबंध, कोड और कविताएं मिलती हैं।', 'AI अगला शब्द इतना अच्छा भांपता है कि वह समझ जैसा दिखता है।', 'किसी AI से एक ही सवाल दो बार पूछिए — थोड़े अलग जवाब संभावना की मशीन दिखा देंगे।', 95, 'hi'),
  ('comm-001-hi', 'STAR तरीका: 60 सेकंड में कोई भी जवाब', 'communication', 50, null, null, 'STAR में R का मतलब क्या है?', array['Reason', 'Response', 'Result', 'Reflection'], 2, '"अपनी किसी असफलता के बारे में बताइए" — इंटरव्यू तोड़ने वाला सवाल। जब तक आप STAR नहीं जानते।', 'STAR = Situation, Task, Action, Result। एक लाइन में संदर्भ, आपकी जिम्मेदारी, आपने खुद क्या किया, और अंत में मापने लायक नतीजा। यह बिखरी बातों को पंचलाइन वाली कहानी बना देता है।', 'अप्रेज़ल में प्रिया: "हमारा कैंपेन पिछड़ रहा था (S)। रीलॉन्च मेरे जिम्मे था (T)। मैंने तीन क्रिएटिव A/B टेस्ट किए और बजट रील्स पर लगाया (A)। दो हफ्तों में CTR 40% बढ़ा (R)।" नब्बे सेकंड, हमेशा याद रहने वाला।', 'Situation, Task, Action, Result — काम की हर कहानी नंबर पर खत्म कीजिए।', 'पिछले महीने के काम से एक STAR कहानी लिखिए — सालों काम आएगी।', 93, 'hi'),
  ('comm-002-hi', 'चुप्पी: मोलभाव का अनदेखा हथियार', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'मोलभाव में अपनी बात कहने के बाद चुप्पी असरदार क्यों है?', array['इससे गुस्सा दिखता है', 'असहजता का दबाव सामने वाले को झुकने या ऑफर बेहतर करने पर मजबूर करता है', 'फोन देखने का समय मिलता है', 'इससे लगता है आपने शर्तें मान लीं'], 1, 'सैलरी ऑफर के बाद जो पहले बोलता है, अक्सर वही हारता है। जानिए क्यों।', 'इंसान चुप्पी भरने की जल्दी में झुक जाता है। अपना आंकड़ा कहने — या उनका सुनने — के बाद बोलना बंद कर दीजिए। चुप्पी का दबाव सामने वाले पर चला जाता है, और वह अक्सर उसे खत्म करने के लिए ऑफर बेहतर कर देता है।', 'HR: "हम ₹12 LPA दे सकते हैं।" आप: "मेरे ऑफर देखते हुए मैं ₹15 की उम्मीद कर रहा था।" फिर चुप्पी। दस असहज सेकंड बाद: "देखता हूं ₹14 हो सकता है क्या।" आपने कुछ नहीं कहा और ₹2 लाख कमा लिए।', 'अपना आंकड़ा कहिए, फिर चुप — चुप्पी आपके लिए मोलभाव करती है।', 'अगली असहमति में जवाब देने से पहले पूरे 5 सेकंड रुकिए।', 91, 'hi'),
  ('prod-001-hi', '2-मिनट नियम टालमटोल खत्म करता है', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'बड़े कामों पर 2-मिनट नियम कैसे लागू होता है?', array['2 खाली घंटे मिलने तक टालिए', 'साथियों में बांट दीजिए', 'शुरुआती कदम को 2 मिनट जितना छोटा कीजिए', '2 मिनट का टाइमर लगाकर घंटी तक काम कीजिए'], 2, 'वह काम आपने 6 बार टाला है। लगते हैं सिर्फ 90 सेकंड। यह रहा इलाज।', 'अगर कोई काम 2 मिनट से कम का है, तुरंत कीजिए — उसे याद रखने, दोबारा पढ़ने और तय करने की लागत करने से ज्यादा है। बड़े कामों के लिए शुरुआत को 2 मिनट का बनाइए: "रिपोर्ट पूरी करो" नहीं, "फाइल खोलो"।', 'प्रोफेसर के ईमेल का जवाब: 2 मिनट — अभी कीजिए। असाइनमेंट लिखना: "डॉक खोलकर एक कच्चा वाक्य लिखो" तक छोटा कीजिए। शुरुआत ही पूरी लड़ाई है; आगे रफ्तार खुद ले जाती है।', '2 मिनट से कम? अभी करो। बहुत बड़ा? शुरुआत को 2 मिनट का बनाओ।', 'इस लेसन के तुरंत बाद तीन 2-मिनट काम निपटाइए।', 92, 'hi'),
  ('prod-006-hi', 'हैबिट स्टैकिंग: पुरानी आदत पर नई आदत', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'हैबिट स्टैकिंग असरदार क्यों है?', array['हर आदत के बाद इनाम मिलता है', 'मौजूदा अपने-आप वाली आदत नई आदत का ट्रिगर बन जाती है', 'यह सुबह के मोटिवेशन पर टिकी है', 'यह ऐप में स्ट्रीक गिनती है'], 1, 'आदत बनाने के लिए मोटिवेशन नहीं चाहिए। एक पहले से मौजूद आदत चाहिए।', 'हैबिट स्टैकिंग नई आदत को किसी मौजूदा अपने-आप होने वाली आदत से जोड़ती है: "जब मैं [मौजूदा आदत] करूं, तब मैं [नई आदत] करूंगा।" पुरानी आदत ट्रिगर बन जाती है — याद रखने या इच्छाशक्ति की जरूरत ही नहीं रहती।', '"सुबह की चाय बनाते ही मैं अपने टॉप 3 काम देखूंगा।" "कॉलेज में स्कूटर लॉक करते ही एक SkillScroll लेसन करूंगा।" कुछ हफ्तों में नई आदत भी पुरानी जितनी अपने-आप चलने लगती है।', '[मौजूदा आदत] के बाद, मैं [नई आदत] करूंगा — नए को अपने-आप वाले से बांधिए।', 'एक हैबिट स्टैक वाक्य लिखकर वहां चिपकाइए जहां पुरानी आदत होती है।', 90, 'hi'),
  ('prod-006', 'Habit stacking: piggyback new habits on old ones', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'What makes habit stacking effective?', array['It uses rewards after each habit', 'An existing automatic behavior becomes the trigger for the new one', 'It relies on strong morning motivation', 'It tracks streaks in an app'], 1, 'You don’t need motivation to build a habit. You need a habit you already have.', 'Habit stacking attaches a new behavior to an existing automatic one: "After I [current habit], I will [new habit]." The old habit becomes the trigger, so you never rely on remembering or willpower.', '"After I pour my morning chai, I’ll review my top 3 tasks." "After I lock my scooter at college, I’ll do one SkillScroll lesson." Within weeks the new behavior fires as automatically as the old one.', 'After [existing habit], I will [new habit] — anchor new to automatic.', 'Write one habit stack sentence and post it where the old habit happens.', 90, 'en'),
  ('fin-007', 'Compound interest and the rule of 72', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'At 12% annual returns, roughly how long does money take to double?', array['3 years', '6 years', '9 years', '12 years'], 1, 'Want to know when your money doubles? Divide 72 by your interest rate. Done.', 'Compound interest pays returns on your returns. The rule of 72 estimates doubling time: 72 ÷ annual return = years to double. The killer insight: each doubling is worth more than all previous ones combined.', '₹1 lakh at 12% doubles every 6 years: ₹2L at 27, ₹4L at 33, ₹8L at 39, ₹16L at 45. The last doubling alone added ₹8 lakh — more than the first three combined. Starting 6 years late costs you the biggest doubling.', '72 ÷ return = years to double. The last doubling is the biggest — start early.', 'Compute how many doublings you get between your age and 60 at 12%.', 91, 'en'),
  ('fin-008', 'Why employer health insurance is not enough', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'What is the core problem with relying only on employer health insurance?', array['It covers only government hospitals', 'It ends when the job ends, and buying later is costly or impossible', 'It cannot cover family members', 'Claims take longer to process'], 1, 'You lose your health cover the same day you lose your job. Read that again.', 'Employer group insurance ends with employment — exactly when you can least afford a hospital bill. A personal health policy bought young is cheap, has no waiting periods left when you need it, and follows you across jobs.', 'A ₹10 lakh personal cover at 25 costs about ₹8,000–10,000/year. Buy it at 45 with a diagnosed condition and it may cost 4x — or be refused. Priya kept her personal policy through two job switches and a startup stint.', 'Employer cover is a bonus, not a plan — buy personal health insurance young.', 'Check whether you have any health cover that survives quitting your job.', 89, 'en'),
  ('fin-009', 'CIBIL score: the number that prices your loans', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'Which habit most protects your credit score?', array['Closing old credit cards quickly', 'Paying every EMI and card bill on time, in full', 'Applying for many cards to increase limits', 'Keeping your salary account and loan at the same bank'], 1, 'Two friends take the same home loan. One pays ₹9 lakh more. The difference: 3 digits.', 'Your CIBIL score (300–900) tells lenders how reliably you repay. Above 750 gets the best rates; below 650 means higher interest or rejection. It is built from payment history, credit utilisation, and account age — and one missed EMI can dent it for years.', 'On a ₹50 lakh, 20-year home loan, a 8.5% rate (score 780) versus 9.4% (score 640) is roughly ₹9 lakh of extra interest. Paying one credit card bill late is the most common cause of the gap.', 'Above 750 = cheap money. Auto-pay every EMI and card bill in full.', 'Pull your free annual CIBIL report and check for errors — they are common.', 90, 'en'),
  ('fin-010', 'PPF vs ELSS: choosing your 80C tax saver', 'finance', 50, null, null, 'What is the lock-in period of ELSS compared to PPF?', array['ELSS 3 years, PPF 15 years', 'ELSS 15 years, PPF 3 years', 'Both 5 years', 'Neither has a lock-in'], 0, 'Both save you the same tax. One may leave you with double the money.', 'Section 80C gives a ₹1.5 lakh deduction. PPF is government-backed, ~7% tax-free, locked 15 years. ELSS is an equity fund, market-linked (~12% long-term average), locked only 3 years. Same tax break — very different growth and risk.', '₹1.5 lakh/year for 15 years: PPF at 7.1% grows to ~₹40 lakh guaranteed. ELSS at 12% grows to ~₹63 lakh, but with market swings along the way. Many split: PPF for safety, ELSS for growth.', 'Same 80C deduction — pick PPF for certainty, ELSS for growth, or split.', 'Check how much of your ₹1.5 lakh 80C limit you actually used last year.', 88, 'en'),
  ('fin-011', 'Lifestyle inflation: the raise that made you poorer', 'finance', 40, null, null, 'What is the most effective defense against lifestyle inflation?', array['Strict monthly budgeting reviews', 'Auto-investing a fixed share of every raise the moment it lands', 'Avoiding all discretionary spending', 'Keeping salary in a separate bank account'], 1, 'Your salary doubled in 4 years. Your savings didn’t move. Where did it go?', 'Lifestyle inflation is spending rising to match every raise — bigger flat, nicer phone, more ordering in. The fix is pre-committing: the moment a raise lands, redirect half of the increase to investments before your lifestyle discovers it.', 'Rahul’s income went from ₹40k to ₹80k. Old rule: save whatever is left (nothing). New rule: of every raise, 50% auto-increases his SIP. His ₹12k SIP became ₹32k without ever feeling poorer — he never had the money in hand.', 'Automate 50% of every raise into investments before you can spend it.', 'Set a reminder for your next appraisal: raise your SIP the same day.', 90, 'en'),
  ('fin-012', 'Your FD is quietly losing money', 'finance', 40, null, null, 'Why can an FD lose purchasing power despite paying interest?', array['Banks secretly deduct maintenance charges', 'Post-tax returns often fall below the inflation rate', 'FD rates change every month', 'FDs are not insured'], 1, 'A 7% FD sounds safe. After tax and inflation, you may be earning nothing.', 'Real return = interest − tax − inflation. FD interest is taxed at your slab rate, so a 7% FD is ~5% post-tax for a 30% slab. With 5–6% inflation, your purchasing power grows ~0% — the money is safe, its value is not.', '₹5 lakh in an FD at 7%: after 30% tax you keep ~4.9%. At 5.5% inflation, one year later your money buys less than 1% more. FDs are parking spots for short-term goals, not engines for long-term wealth.', 'FDs preserve money for the short term; equity grows it for the long term.', 'Compute your FD’s post-tax return and subtract this year’s inflation.', 87, 'en'),
  ('tech-007', 'The padlock: what HTTPS actually protects', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'What does HTTPS guarantee?', array['The website is government-approved', 'The connection is encrypted and the server is who it claims to be', 'The website has no malware', 'Your data will never be stored'], 1, 'On café Wi-Fi, anyone can read your traffic. Except the parts behind the padlock.', 'HTTPS encrypts data between your device and the website, so people on the same network — or your ISP — see only gibberish. It also verifies you are talking to the real site, not an imposter. It does NOT mean the site itself is trustworthy.', 'On airport Wi-Fi, logging into your bank over HTTPS keeps your password unreadable to snoopers. But a phishing site can also have a padlock — HTTPS proves the connection is private, not that the destination is honest.', 'The padlock means a private connection — not a trustworthy website.', 'Check the URL, not just the padlock, before entering any password.', 89, 'en'),
  ('tech-008', 'Why free apps want your contacts', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'What is the safest default approach to app permissions?', array['Grant everything so apps work smoothly', 'Deny by default and grant only when a feature actually needs it', 'Only grant permissions to popular apps', 'Permissions do not matter on iPhones'], 1, 'A flashlight app asking for your contact list is not confused. It’s a business model.', 'Free apps often monetize data: contacts, location, and usage patterns feed advertising profiles worth more than a purchase price. Permissions are the negotiation — every grant is a trade of privacy for convenience, and most apps work fine without the extras.', 'A photo editor requesting location and contacts can build a profile of where you live and who you know. Deny both — it still edits photos. Android and iOS both let you grant location "only while using" or approximate instead of precise.', 'Every permission is a trade. Deny first — grant only when a feature breaks.', 'Open app permissions in Settings and revoke location from three apps.', 88, 'en'),
  ('tech-009', 'Open source: free software runs the world', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'What is a key security advantage of open-source software?', array['It has no bugs', 'Anyone can inspect the code, so flaws are found and fixed faster', 'It cannot be hacked because it is free', 'Governments certify all open-source projects'], 1, 'The code behind your bank, your phone, and this app was mostly written for free. On purpose.', 'Open-source software publishes its code for anyone to inspect, use, and improve. Linux, Android’s core, and most of the internet’s servers are open source. Openness means thousands of eyes find bugs, and no single company can lock you in.', 'UPI processes billions of transactions on infrastructure full of open-source components. When a security bug appears in a popular library, researchers worldwide can see and fix it — closed code waits for one vendor to notice.', 'Open source wins because transparency scales trust and talent.', 'Look up one app you use daily and check what open-source projects it credits.', 87, 'en'),
  ('tech-010', 'Databases: why companies don’t run on Excel', 'technology', 50, null, null, 'What does a database transaction guarantee?', array['Data is always encrypted', 'Multi-step changes either fully complete or fully roll back', 'Queries always run in under a second', 'Data is backed up daily'], 1, 'Excel dies at a million rows. Your bank has billions. Here’s what they use instead.', 'A database stores data in tables built for scale: millions of reads/writes per second, rules that keep data valid, and transactions that make multi-step changes all-or-nothing — so money never vanishes between two accounts mid-transfer.', 'When you pay ₹500 by UPI, a transaction debits you and credits the shop as one atomic unit. If the power fails midway, the database rolls both back. A spreadsheet would happily save half the operation.', 'Databases = spreadsheets with guarantees: scale, rules, and all-or-nothing changes.', 'Notice one app action today that must be all-or-nothing — that’s a transaction.', 90, 'en'),
  ('tech-011', 'Why SMS OTP is the weakest second factor', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'Why are authenticator apps safer than SMS OTPs?', array['They generate longer codes', 'Codes never travel over the phone network, so SIM-swap fraud can’t intercept them', 'They work without a password', 'Banks monitor them more closely'], 1, 'Scammers don’t hack your password. They convince your telecom to hand them your number.', 'Two-factor authentication adds "something you have" to your password. SMS OTPs are the weakest form: SIM-swap fraud, phishing pages that relay codes in real time, and shoulder-surfing all defeat them. Authenticator apps and passkeys are phishing-resistant upgrades.', 'A fraudster gets a duplicate SIM issued with forged documents; your phone goes dead, theirs receives your bank OTPs. With an authenticator app or passkey, the code never travels over the phone network at all.', 'Any 2FA beats none — but prefer authenticator apps or passkeys over SMS.', 'Switch one important account from SMS OTP to an authenticator app.', 91, 'en'),
  ('tech-012', 'Why your feed knows you too well', 'technology', 50, null, null, 'How do recommendation algorithms mainly infer your interests?', array['By reading your private messages', 'From behavioral signals like watch time, pauses, and replays', 'From your phone’s microphone', 'By asking advertisers what you bought'], 1, 'You never told Instagram you were thinking of quitting your job. It knows anyway.', 'Recommendation algorithms don’t read minds — they read behavior. Every pause, replay, and half-typed search is a signal. Millions of users with similar signal patterns reveal what you’ll likely want next, often before you consciously know it.', 'Linger two extra seconds on career-change reels and the algorithm quietly tests more of them. Engage again, and your feed tilts within a day. Nobody labeled you — the pattern of people who paused like you did the labeling.', 'Feeds model your behavior, not your words — attention is the vote.', 'Deliberately linger on three topics you WANT more of — train your feed on purpose.', 92, 'en'),
  ('comm-007', 'The 30-second elevator pitch', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'What are the three beats of an effective elevator pitch?', array['Education, hobbies, salary expectations', 'Who you are, one concrete proof, one clear ask', 'Strengths, weaknesses, opportunities', 'Past, present, future in equal detail'], 1, '"So, tell me about yourself." You have 30 seconds. Most people waste 25 of them.', 'A pitch has three beats: who you are (one line), what you’ve done that matters (one concrete proof), and what you want next (one ask). Cut credentials nobody asked for; keep the proof specific and the ask clear.', '"I’m a final-year B.Com student who runs a 12k-follower finance page — my reel on SIPs crossed 300k views. I’m looking for a content internship at a fintech." Twelve seconds, memorable, actionable.', 'Who you are, one proof, one ask — in under 30 seconds.', 'Write your three beats and say them aloud twice — cut anything past 30 seconds.', 90, 'en'),
  ('comm-008', 'Give feedback with SBI, not a sandwich', 'communication', 50, null, null, 'In SBI feedback, what should you describe instead of judging the person?', array['Their intentions', 'Their personality traits', 'The observable behavior and its impact', 'How you would have done it'], 2, 'Praise-criticism-praise trains people to fear your compliments. There’s a better way.', 'SBI = Situation, Behavior, Impact. Name the specific situation, describe the observable behavior (not the person), and state its impact. No character judgments, no buried criticism — just a repeatable, non-threatening structure.', 'Instead of "You’re careless": "In yesterday’s client call (S), the deck had last month’s numbers (B), and the client questioned our accuracy (I)." The person can fix a behavior; they can only resent a label.', 'Describe the situation and behavior, state the impact — never label the person.', 'Rewrite one piece of feedback you need to give in SBI form.', 91, 'en'),
  ('comm-009', 'Small talk without the awkwardness: FORD', 'communication', 40, null, null, 'What does the FORD method suggest you do after asking a question?', array['Move to the next FORD topic quickly', 'Share your own story on the same topic', 'Go deeper on whichever topic energizes the other person', 'Change to professional topics'], 2, 'Stuck after "so… how’s it going?" Four topics work with literally anyone.', 'FORD = Family, Occupation, Recreation, Dreams. Everyone can talk about at least one. Ask open questions ("what’s keeping you busy these days?"), then follow the energy — go deeper on whatever lights them up.', 'At a cousin’s wedding, Arjun asks an uncle: "What are you enjoying most since retiring?" (Occupation→Recreation). Twenty minutes later the uncle is explaining his terrace garden and offering internship contacts. People remember who made them talk.', 'Family, Occupation, Recreation, Dreams — ask open, follow the energy.', 'Use one FORD question on someone you’d normally just nod at.', 86, 'en'),
  ('comm-010', 'Speak in the first five minutes of every meeting', 'communication', 40, null, null, 'Why does speaking early in a meeting matter?', array['It impresses the most senior person', 'Silence compounds — the longer you wait, the harder contributing becomes', 'Early comments are recorded in minutes', 'It prevents others from speaking'], 1, 'The longer you stay silent in a meeting, the harder speaking becomes. It’s physics.', 'Silence compounds: after 20 quiet minutes, your brain inflates the stakes of finally speaking. Break the seal early with a low-stakes contribution — a clarifying question, agreeing and adding one point. Early voices get attributed the ideas.', 'Priya’s rule: one comment before minute five, even just "Quick question — is the goal signups or revenue?" After adopting it, her manager’s feedback changed from "be more visible" to "drives discussions".', 'Say something low-stakes in the first 5 minutes — silence compounds.', 'In your next meeting, ask one clarifying question before minute five.', 89, 'en'),
  ('comm-011', 'Data needs a story: the 3-part work narrative', 'communication', 50, null, null, 'What is the "surprise" in a data narrative?', array['An unexpected chart type', 'The gap between what was believed and what the data shows', 'A dramatic pause before revealing numbers', 'Withholding the conclusion until the end'], 1, 'Nobody remembers your dashboard. Everyone remembers what it meant.', 'Numbers persuade only inside a narrative: what we believed (setup), what the data showed (surprise), what we should do (resolution). The surprise is the hook — lead with the gap between expectation and reality.', '"We assumed users churned over price (setup). Exit surveys show 60% left because onboarding confused them (surprise). A guided first session could cut churn by a third (resolution)." Same data as the dashboard — now it moves a decision.', 'Setup, surprise, resolution — lead with the gap between belief and data.', 'Take one number from your work and wrap it in the 3-part narrative.', 90, 'en'),
  ('comm-012', 'Your posture speaks before you do', 'communication', 40, null, null, 'How does body language affect how your words are received?', array['It only matters on video calls', 'It sets the frame of confidence or disinterest your words are interpreted in', 'It matters only for senior roles', 'It has no measurable effect'], 1, 'Interviewers form an impression in 7 seconds. You haven’t said a word yet.', 'Body language sets the frame your words are heard in: open posture, steady eye contact, and unhurried movement read as confidence; crossed arms, phone-checking, and fidgeting read as disinterest — regardless of what you say.', 'Two candidates give identical answers. One sits upright, hands visible, nodding while listening; the other slouches and glances at the clock. Panels consistently rate the first "more confident" — same words, different frame.', 'Open posture, steady eyes, slow movements — set the frame before you speak.', 'In your next conversation, keep both hands visible and uncross your arms.', 86, 'en'),
  ('prod-007', 'Parkinson’s law: shrink the container', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'What does Parkinson’s law state?', array['Work expands to fill the time available for it', 'Productivity doubles every two years', '80% of results come from 20% of effort', 'Interruptions cost 23 minutes each'], 0, 'Given a week, the task takes a week. Given a day, it takes a day. Same task.', 'Parkinson’s law: work expands to fill the time available. Deadlines are containers — the task shapes itself to fit. The lever: set aggressive artificial deadlines and let the constraint force prioritization of what actually matters.', 'A college fest poster "due next week" absorbs 6 days of tinkering. The same poster before a 7pm print slot gets done in 3 focused hours — and nobody can tell the difference. The deadline did the editing.', 'Work fills its container — give tasks smaller containers.', 'Pick one task and halve its deadline. Notice what you stop bothering with.', 89, 'en'),
  ('prod-008', 'The Eisenhower matrix: urgent is not important', 'productivity', 50, null, null, 'Which quadrant do people most neglect, per the Eisenhower matrix?', array['Urgent and important', 'Important but not urgent', 'Urgent but not important', 'Neither urgent nor important'], 1, 'Your whole day was urgent. None of it was important. That’s why you feel busy and behind.', 'Sort tasks on two axes: urgent/not, important/not. Urgent+important: do now. Important+not urgent: schedule — this quadrant builds your future and is the first casualty of a reactive day. Urgent+not important: delegate or batch. Neither: delete.', 'Replying to every ping is urgent-not-important; learning Excel properly is important-not-urgent. Priya blocks 8–9am for quadrant two before the urgent day begins — the skills that got her promoted all came from that hour.', 'Protect important-but-not-urgent time — it’s where your future is built.', 'Sort today’s list into the four quadrants. Schedule one quadrant-two block.', 91, 'en'),
  ('prod-009', 'Manage energy, not time', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'What does energy management recommend for low-energy periods?', array['Push through hard tasks with caffeine', 'Schedule shallow work like email and admin', 'Take the rest of the day off', 'Switch to a standing desk'], 1, 'You had 3 free hours last night and achieved nothing. The hours weren’t the problem.', 'An hour of peak energy outproduces three depleted ones. Map your energy curve — most people peak mid-morning and dip after lunch — then match task difficulty to energy level: creative work at peaks, email and admin in troughs.', 'Rahul moved client design work from 10pm (exhausted) to 8–10am (fresh) and admin to the 3pm slump. Same total hours; output nearly doubled. Night-time became reading, not guilt.', 'Schedule hard work at your energy peaks, shallow work in the troughs.', 'Note your energy at 10am, 2pm, and 6pm. Plan tomorrow’s hardest task at the peak.', 88, 'en'),
  ('prod-010', 'Multitasking is a myth your brain tells you', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'What actually happens in the brain during "multitasking"?', array['Both tasks run in parallel at slightly lower quality', 'Rapid switching between tasks, each switch costing time and errors', 'The subconscious handles the second task', 'Nothing — trained people multitask fine'], 1, 'You’re not doing two things at once. You’re doing two things badly, alternately.', 'The brain doesn’t parallel-process demanding tasks — it switches, and every switch pays a cost in errors and refocus time. "Multitasking" is rapid task-switching with up to 40% productivity loss, felt as busyness.', 'Writing a report while answering chat feels efficient. Measured: the report takes 60% longer and contains more errors than writing it in one closed-tab hour and then batching every reply. Same work, one switch instead of forty.', 'Single-task in blocks, batch the interruptions — switching is the real cost.', 'Do one task for 30 minutes with every other tab and app closed.', 90, 'en'),
  ('prod-011', 'The weekly review: 30 minutes that run your week', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'What is the final step of a weekly review?', array['Archiving old emails', 'Choosing next week’s key outcomes and putting them on the calendar', 'Writing a diary entry about the week', 'Planning every hour of the coming week'], 1, 'Monday-morning chaos isn’t a time problem. It’s a Sunday problem.', 'A weekly review is 30 minutes, same time each week: clear loose ends (inbox, notes, chats), review what moved and what stalled, then pick the 3 outcomes that define next week and put them on the calendar first.', 'Sunday 6pm: Arjun empties his notes app, sees the accounts project stalled two weeks running, and books Tuesday + Thursday mornings for it before anything else claims them. Monday starts with decisions already made.', '30 minutes weekly: clear loose ends, review, pick 3 outcomes, calendar them.', 'Block a recurring 30-minute weekly review slot right now.', 88, 'en'),
  ('prod-012', 'The notification audit: attention is opt-in', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'What is the key test in a notification audit?', array['Does the app have more than a million users?', 'Has this notification ever usefully changed your next five minutes?', 'Is the app free or paid?', 'Does the notification arrive during work hours?'], 1, 'You check your phone 96 times a day. You chose almost none of those checks.', 'Every notification is an app claiming your attention on its schedule. The audit: for each app, ask "has this notification ever changed what I did in the next five minutes for the better?" If not, silence it. Keep humans, kill algorithms.', 'Rahul silenced everything except calls, messages from real people, and payment alerts — 9 apps lost push rights. His screen pickups dropped by a third in a week, and he missed exactly nothing that mattered.', 'Keep notifications from humans, silence notifications from algorithms.', 'Turn off notifications for the three apps that interrupted you most today.', 89, 'en'),
  ('fin-013', 'The subscription leak: audit your autopay', 'finance', 40, null, null, 'Why do subscription leaks happen so easily?', array['Banks hide the charges', 'Small auto-renewing amounts are designed to be forgotten', 'Subscriptions cannot be cancelled online', 'UPI mandates expire automatically'], 1, 'You are paying for 3 apps you forgot exist. So is almost everyone.', 'Subscriptions are designed to be forgotten: small monthly amounts, auto-renewal, and silent UPI mandates. A twice-yearly audit of active mandates and card autopays typically recovers ₹500–1,500/month — a raise you give yourself in 15 minutes.', 'Priya’s audit found: an OTT platform she left in January (₹299), a fitness app from a resolution (₹199), and a cloud plan doubled-up with her phone brand’s (₹130). ₹628/month = ₹7,536/year, redirected to her SIP.', 'Audit UPI mandates and card autopays twice a year — cancel the forgotten.', 'Open your UPI app’s mandates list and cancel one subscription you don’t use.', 88, 'en'),
  ('fin-014', 'Buying gold: jewellery vs SGB vs ETF', 'finance', 50, null, null, 'What extra return do Sovereign Gold Bonds add over the gold price?', array['Nothing extra', '2.5% yearly interest', 'A 10% maturity bonus', 'Free making charges'], 1, 'Buying gold jewellery as an "investment" loses 20% the moment you walk out of the shop.', 'Jewellery carries making charges (8–25%) and GST you never recover. Gold ETFs track the metal price with tiny fees and instant liquidity. Sovereign Gold Bonds add 2.5% yearly interest on top of the gold price and are tax-free at maturity — the best deal for long holders.', '₹1 lakh in jewellery = ~₹80–90k of actual gold after charges. The same in SGBs = ₹1 lakh of gold exposure + ₹2,500/year interest + zero capital gains tax after 8 years. Same metal, very different outcomes.', 'Wear jewellery; invest through SGBs or ETFs.', 'Check the making charges on your last jewellery bill — that’s the entry loss.', 89, 'en'),
  ('fin-015', 'No-cost EMI: the cost is hidden, not absent', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'How does "no-cost EMI" actually charge you?', array['It doesn’t — retailers absorb the cost', 'Through foregone discounts, processing fees, and GST on embedded interest', 'Through a higher credit score requirement', 'By extending the warranty period'], 1, '"No-cost EMI" is the most profitable phrase in Indian retail. Guess who profits.', 'No-cost EMI hides its cost three ways: the discount you’d get on full payment vanishes, a processing fee plus GST on interest is charged, and the EMI habit normalizes buying beyond your means. The interest exists — it’s baked into the price or foregone discount.', 'A ₹60,000 phone: cash buyers get it for ₹57,000 with an instant discount. The "no-cost" EMI buyer pays ₹60,000 plus a ₹500 processing fee and GST on the embedded interest. The EMI cost ₹3,700 — it just never appeared as "interest".', 'If you can’t buy it twice in cash, the EMI isn’t the problem — the purchase is.', 'Before your next EMI checkout, ask for the full-payment price and compare.', 90, 'en'),
  ('tech-013', 'Cookies: how websites remember you', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'What makes third-party cookies a privacy concern?', array['They can carry viruses', 'They let one ad network recognize you across many different sites', 'They slow down page loading', 'They expire too quickly'], 1, 'You looked at one pair of shoes. Now they follow you across the entire internet. Here’s how.', 'A cookie is a small note a website stores in your browser and reads back on the next visit — that’s how logins persist and carts survive. Third-party cookies are notes readable across many sites, letting ad networks stitch your browsing into one profile.', 'The shoe store sets its own cookie (your cart) and an ad network’s cookie. Later, a news site loads the same ad network, which reads its cookie and recognizes you — hence the shoes in the sidebar. Blocking third-party cookies breaks the trail, not your logins.', 'First-party cookies remember you for the site; third-party ones follow you across sites.', 'Turn on "block third-party cookies" in your browser — your logins will survive.', 88, 'en'),
  ('tech-014', 'VPNs: what they hide and what they don’t', 'technology', 50, null, null, 'What does a VPN actually protect?', array['Your identity on sites you log into', 'Traffic between you and the VPN server, and your IP from websites', 'Your accounts from being hacked', 'Your device from malware'], 1, 'A VPN ad says you’re "invisible online". You’re not. Here’s what actually changes.', 'A VPN encrypts traffic between you and the VPN server, hiding it from your Wi-Fi network and ISP, and masking your IP from websites. It does NOT hide you from the sites you log into, doesn’t stop cookies or fingerprinting, and shifts trust from your ISP to the VPN company.', 'On hostel Wi-Fi, a VPN stops the network admin seeing which sites you visit. But log into Instagram through it and Instagram still knows exactly who you are — you told it. And a free VPN may sell the very browsing data you tried to protect.', 'A VPN moves your trust, it doesn’t erase your identity — logins still identify you.', 'If you use a free VPN, read its privacy policy’s data-sharing section.', 89, 'en'),
  ('tech-015', 'Spot a phishing message in 5 seconds', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'What is the common fingerprint of phishing messages?', array['Poor grammar only', 'Manufactured urgency plus an immediate action like clicking or sharing an OTP', 'They always come from international numbers', 'They only arrive on email, never SMS'], 1, '"Your electricity will be disconnected tonight. Call immediately." Delete it. Here’s why.', 'Phishing has one fingerprint: urgency + an action only you can take right now (click, call, pay, share OTP). Real institutions never threaten same-day consequences over SMS, never ask for OTPs, and their links match their official domain exactly.', '"KYC expires today, click bit.ly/xyz" fails all three checks: artificial deadline, shortened link, and banks update KYC in-branch or in-app, never via SMS links. The 5-second test: would this message still make sense if you called the official number instead?', 'Urgency + link + secrecy = phishing. Verify through the official app, never the message.', 'Find one suspicious message in your inbox and check the sender’s real domain.', 92, 'en'),
  ('comm-013', 'Ask for a raise: preparation beats courage', 'communication', 55, null, null, 'What should you bring to a raise conversation?', array['A list of personal expenses that increased', 'Documented impact with numbers, market data, and a specific ask', 'Competing offers only', 'A resignation letter as leverage'], 1, 'The raise conversation is won two weeks before it happens. Most people prepare for two minutes.', 'A raise ask needs three artifacts: a one-page impact list with numbers (what you delivered), market data (what the role pays elsewhere), and a specific number (never "whatever you think is fair"). Deliver it in a scheduled meeting, not a corridor.', '"Over 12 months I shipped the onboarding revamp (+18% activation) and took over vendor management (₹4L saved). Market range for my role is 14–17 LPA; I’m at 11. I’d like to discuss moving to 15." Numbers make it a business decision, not a favour.', 'Impact list + market data + a specific number, in a scheduled meeting.', 'Start your impact list now — add one bullet with a number from this quarter.', 91, 'en'),
  ('comm-014', 'Disagree without being disagreeable', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'What is the first step of productive disagreement?', array['Stating your counter-argument clearly', 'Restating the other person’s view to show you understood it', 'Finding an authority who agrees with you', 'Asking to discuss it privately later'], 1, 'The person who disagrees best in the room is usually the most trusted. Not the loudest — the best.', 'Productive disagreement separates the idea from the person: restate their view first ("If I understand, you’re saying…"), name what you agree with, then place your concern on the idea, not the speaker — and offer a testable alternative.', 'Instead of "That won’t work": "You’re proposing launching in all cities at once — I agree speed matters. My concern is support load; last launch, tickets tripled. Could we pilot in two cities for two weeks first?" Disagreement becomes collaboration.', 'Restate, agree where true, aim concerns at the idea, offer a testable alternative.', 'In your next disagreement, restate the other side’s view before yours.', 90, 'en'),
  ('comm-015', 'The follow-up: where opportunities actually convert', 'communication', 40, null, null, 'What makes a follow-up effective rather than annoying?', array['Sending it within an hour of the first message', 'Adding new value and making the next step effortless', 'Mentioning how many times you’ve already written', 'Copying the person’s manager'], 1, 'The internship went to the slightly-worse candidate who followed up. It usually does.', 'Most asks die not from rejection but from silence — busy people forget. A good follow-up is short, adds one new piece of value (a relevant link, an updated portfolio piece), and makes the next step effortless. Two follow-ups spaced a week apart is persistence; five in a day is spam.', '"Hi Ananya, following up on the design internship — since we spoke I redesigned a food app’s checkout flow (attached, 2 screens). Happy to do a 15-min call whenever suits you." New value, low effort to reply, zero guilt-tripping.', 'Follow up twice, a week apart, each time adding one new piece of value.', 'Send one follow-up on something that went silent — attach one new thing.', 88, 'en'),
  ('prod-013', 'Batching: context is the expensive part', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'Why does batching similar tasks save time?', array['Tasks become easier with repetition', 'The mental context loads once per batch instead of once per task', 'It impresses managers with visible focus', 'Batched tasks can be delegated more easily'], 1, 'Answering 20 emails takes 25 minutes — or 3 hours. Same emails. The difference is when.', 'Every task type needs a mental context that takes minutes to load. Batching groups same-context tasks — all emails at 12 and 5, all calls back-to-back, all errands in one trip — so you pay the context-loading cost once per batch instead of once per task.', 'Rahul answered client messages as they arrived: 20 interruptions, each derailing design work for 10+ minutes. Now messages get two 20-minute windows a day. Clients noticed nothing; his deep work hours doubled.', 'Group same-context tasks into windows — pay the switching cost once.', 'Pick two fixed times tomorrow for all messages. Outside them, stay closed.', 89, 'en'),
  ('prod-014', 'The 25/5 list: your goals are fighting each other', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'In the 25/5 method, how should you treat goals 6 through 25?', array['Work on them during weekends', 'Actively avoid them until the top 5 are achieved', 'Delegate them to others', 'Review them monthly for promotion to the top 5'], 1, 'Write your top 25 goals. Circle 5. Buffett’s advice for the other 20: avoid at all costs.', 'The 25/5 method exposes the real enemy of your top goals: your medium goals. Items 6–25 are appealing enough to steal time but not important enough to change your life. They deserve active avoidance, not "later" — later is where priority goes to die.', 'Arjun’s list: CA prep, fitness, finance content page, guitar, video editing, 20 more. Guitar and editing were "sort-of goals" eating his CA study evenings. Naming them as avoid-list items ended the guilt — he wasn’t quitting them, he was sequencing them.', 'Your top 5 goals aren’t threatened by distractions — they’re threatened by goals 6–25.', 'Write the 25, circle 5, and label the rest "avoid until further notice".', 91, 'en'),
  ('prod-015', 'Sleep is a productivity tool, not its enemy', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'What happens to the day’s learning during sleep?', array['It fades unless revised immediately on waking', 'It gets consolidated into long-term memory', 'Nothing — sleep only affects physical energy', 'It transfers only if you dream about it'], 1, 'Pulling an all-nighter to be productive is like burning furniture to heat the house.', 'Under 6 hours of sleep measurably cuts focus, memory formation, and decision quality the entire next day — the work you gain at 1am is repaid with interest as slow, error-prone hours. Consistency matters more than duration: same sleep and wake time daily beats weekend catch-up.', 'Two students before exams: one studies till 3am and re-reads the same page five times the next day; the other stops at 11pm — sleep consolidates the day’s studying into long-term memory overnight. The second one literally learns while sleeping.', 'Protect 7+ hours at fixed times — sleep consolidates what you studied today.', 'Set a "start winding down" alarm 45 minutes before your target bedtime.', 90, 'en'),
  ('fin-006-hi', 'सैलरी के लिए 50-30-20 नियम', 'finance', 40, null, null, '50-30-20 नियम में 20% किसके लिए है?', array['किराया और बिल', 'मनोरंजन और शॉपिंग', 'बचत और निवेश', 'टैक्स'], 2, '20 तारीख तक सैलरी खत्म? आपको ज्यादा कमाई नहीं, तीन बकेट चाहिए।', 'टेक-होम सैलरी को बांटिए: 50% जरूरतें (किराया, खाना, आना-जाना), 30% शौक (बाहर खाना, शॉपिंग), 20% बचत/निवेश। यह 20% सैलरी आते ही निकल जाए — इससे पहले कि आप खर्च कर सकें।', '₹60,000 टेक-होम पर: ₹30,000 जरूरतें, ₹18,000 शौक, ₹12,000 महीने की 1 तारीख को अपने-आप SIP + इमरजेंसी फंड में। खर्च बचे हुए में ढलता है — उल्टा नहीं।', 'पहले बचत, फिर बचे हुए का खर्च — कभी उल्टा नहीं।', 'सैलरी के अगले दिन के लिए 20% का ऑटो-ट्रांसफर सेट कीजिए।', 87, 'hi'),
  ('fin-004-hi', 'क्रेडिट कार्ड ब्याज: 42% का जाल', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'बकाया राशि पर क्रेडिट कार्ड सालाना लगभग कितना ब्याज लेते हैं?', array['8–10%', '15–18%', '24–30%', '36–45%'], 3, '"मिनिमम अमाउंट ड्यू" आपके फोन का सबसे महंगा बटन है।', 'क्रेडिट कार्ड बकाया राशि पर ~3.5% महीना — यानी सालाना करीब 42% — ब्याज लेते हैं। सिर्फ मिनिमम ड्यू भरने से कर्ज सालों जिंदा रहता है और ब्याज आपके खिलाफ चक्रवृद्धि होता रहता है।', '₹50,000 का बिल 5% मिनिमम ड्यू पर चुकाने में 6 साल से ज्यादा लगते हैं और ~₹48,000 ब्याज जाता है — यानी आपकी हर खरीद लगभग दोगुनी कीमत की हो गई।', 'हर महीने पूरा बिल भरिए, वरना कार्ड मत घुमाइए।', 'ऑटो-पे को "मिनिमम ड्यू" से बदलकर "टोटल ड्यू" कीजिए।', 91, 'hi'),
  ('tech-004-hi', '"क्लाउड" असल में क्या है', 'technology', 40, null, null, 'क्लाउड कंप्यूटिंग का मुख्य आर्थिक फायदा क्या है?', array['डेटा अपने सर्वर से ज्यादा सुरक्षित रहता है', 'सिर्फ इस्तेमाल जितनी क्षमता का भुगतान और तुरंत घटा-बढ़ा सकते हैं', 'यह बिना इंटरनेट चलता है', 'ऐप्स अपने-आप तेज हो जाते हैं'], 1, 'आपकी फोटो आसमान में नहीं तैर रहीं। वे मुंबई की एक इमारत में हैं, जहां AC बहुत अच्छा है।', 'क्लाउड यानी दूसरों के कंप्यूटर — विशाल डेटा सेंटर जिन्हें आप सेकंड के हिसाब से किराए पर लेते हैं। सर्वर खरीदने की जगह कंपनियां AWS, Google या Azure को सिर्फ इस्तेमाल जितना देती हैं और जरूरत पर तुरंत घटा-बढ़ा लेती हैं।', 'Hotstar IPL के 2.5 करोड़ दर्शकों के लिए मैच के घंटों में हजारों अतिरिक्त क्लाउड सर्वर किराए पर लेता है और रात में छोड़ देता है। रोज 4 घंटे के पीक के लिए वह हार्डवेयर खरीदना बर्बादी होती।', 'क्लाउड = बिजली की तरह किराए की कंप्यूटिंग — जितना इस्तेमाल, उतना भुगतान।', 'देखिए आपका फोन फोटो कहां बैकअप करता है — वह डेटा सेंटर है, आसमान नहीं।', 88, 'hi'),
  ('tech-006-hi', 'फ्रंटएंड बनाम बैकएंड: एक मेट्रो यात्रा में', 'technology', 40, null, null, 'इनमें से बैकएंड की जिम्मेदारी कौन-सी है?', array['बटन के एनिमेशन', 'पेमेंट प्रोसेस करना और टिकट सहेजना', 'ऐप की रंग योजना', 'वापस जाने का स्वाइप जेस्चर'], 1, 'जो मेट्रो स्टेशन आप देखते हैं, वह मेट्रो का 10% है। बाकी 90% तय करता है कि ट्रेन आएगी या नहीं।', 'फ्रंटएंड वह सब है जो यूज़र देखता और छूता है — स्क्रीन, बटन, एनिमेशन। बैकएंड छिपी मशीनरी है — डेटाबेस, सर्वर, पेमेंट, लॉजिक। दोनों API से बात करते हैं।', 'Paytm पर मेट्रो टिकट: फ्रंटएंड नक्शा और पे बटन दिखाता है। बैकएंड किराया जांचता है, बैंक से बात करता है, QR कोड बनाता है और टिकट सहेजता है — सब अदृश्य।', 'फ्रंटएंड दिखने वाला स्टेशन है; बैकएंड ट्रेनें चलाने वाला नेटवर्क।', 'अगली बार ऐप में स्पिनर दिखे, तो समझिए फ्रंटएंड बैकएंड का इंतजार कर रहा है।', 86, 'hi'),
  ('comm-003-hi', 'ऐसे ईमेल लिखिए जिनका जवाब आए', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'काम के ईमेल में मुख्य मांग कहां होनी चाहिए?', array['अंत में, पूरे संदर्भ के बाद', 'पहली लाइन में', 'सिर्फ सब्जेक्ट में', 'फॉलो-अप ईमेल में'], 1, 'आपका 8 पैराग्राफ का ईमेल कभी पढ़ा ही नहीं गया। जो जवाब चाहिए था, वह 4 लाइन में आ जाता।', 'व्यस्त लोग पढ़ते नहीं, स्कैन करते हैं। पहली लाइन में अपनी मांग रखिए, सिर्फ फैसले लायक संदर्भ दीजिए, और अंत में एक साफ सवाल + डेडलाइन। एक ईमेल, एक मांग।', 'लंबे अपडेट में दबी विनती की जगह: "हाय रवि — दिवाली विज्ञापन के ₹80k बजट पर शुक्रवार तक आपकी मंजूरी चाहिए। संदर्भ: पिछले साल ₹60k पर 3x रिटर्न मिला। मंजूर, या पहले कॉल करें?" जवाब मिनटों में, दिनों में नहीं।', 'पहले मांग, फिर संदर्भ, एक सवाल, एक डेडलाइन।', 'अगला ईमेल ऐसे लिखिए कि पहला वाक्य ही आपकी मांग हो।', 89, 'hi'),
  ('comm-006-hi', 'रिश्ते बिगाड़े बिना ना कहना', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'शालीन ना के तीन हिस्से क्या हैं?', array['माफी, लंबी सफाई, अगली बार का वादा', 'कद्र, कारण के साथ मना, विकल्प का प्रस्ताव', 'नजरअंदाज करो, टालो, भूलने की उम्मीद करो', 'हां कहो, फिर बाद में रद्द करो'], 1, 'हर "छोटा-सा काम" जो आप मानते हैं, अपने काम को एक खामोश ना है। विनम्र ना सीखिए।', 'अच्छी ना के तीन हिस्से: पूछने की कद्र कीजिए, संक्षिप्त सच्चे कारण के साथ मना कीजिए, और विकल्प दीजिए (बाद की तारीख, कोई और व्यक्ति, छोटा दायरा)। समय भी बचता है, रिश्ता भी।', '"फेस्ट कमेटी के लिए मुझे सोचा, शुक्रिया — इस महीने नहीं हो पाएगा, फाइनल हैं। हां, रविवार तक आपकी स्पॉन्सरशिप डेक देख सकता हूं, चलेगा?" न अपराधबोध, न कड़वाहट, रिश्ता कायम।', 'कद्र कीजिए, कारण से मना कीजिए, विकल्प दीजिए — ना भरोसा भी बढ़ा सकती है।', 'आज एक कम जरूरी काम तीन-हिस्सों वाले तरीके से मना कीजिए।', 88, 'hi'),
  ('prod-002-hi', 'डीप वर्क: 90 मिनट, 8 घंटों पर भारी', 'productivity', 50, null, null, 'रुकावट के बाद दोबारा एकाग्र होने में लगभग कितना समय लगता है?', array['30 सेकंड', '5 मिनट', '23 मिनट', '2 घंटे'], 2, 'कल आपने 8 घंटे काम किया और नतीजा लगभग शून्य रहा। वजह यह है।', 'हर नोटिफिकेशन ~23 मिनट की एकाग्रता खा जाता है। डीप वर्क यानी 90 मिनट का एक ब्लॉक: फोन दूसरे कमरे में, एक काम, एक टैब। 90 एकाग्र मिनट अक्सर पूरे बिखरे दिन से ज्यादा काम निकालते हैं।', 'राहुल के क्लाइंट डिज़ाइन 3 बिखरे दिनों में बनते थे। उसने सुबह के दो 90-मिनट ब्लॉक शुरू किए, फोन रसोई में। वही डिज़ाइन अब एक दिन में — दोपहर खाली।', 'एक 90-मिनट ब्लॉक, फोन दूसरे कमरे में — वही असली वर्कडे है।', 'कल सुबह 90 मिनट ब्लॉक कीजिए; फोन शारीरिक रूप से कहीं और रखिए।', 91, 'hi'),
  ('prod-003-hi', 'सबसे कठिन काम सबसे पहले', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'सबसे कठिन काम दिन में सबसे पहले क्यों करना चाहिए?', array['सुबह ऑफिस शांत रहता है', 'इच्छाशक्ति और एकाग्रता सुबह सबसे ऊंची होती है', 'इससे मैनेजर प्रभावित होता है', 'आसान काम लंच के बाद बेहतर होते हैं'], 1, 'आपकी टू-डू लिस्ट लंबी नहीं है। वह गलत क्रम में है।', '"ईट द फ्रॉग" यानी सबसे कठिन, सबसे टाला हुआ काम दिन की शुरुआत में करना, जब इच्छाशक्ति चरम पर होती है। उसके बाद सब आसान लगता है, और दिन भर खाए जाने वाला बोझ सुबह 10 बजे तक गायब।', 'अर्जुन "अकाउंट्स चैप्टर पढ़ना" आसान कामों के नीचे रखता था। क्रम पलटा: सुबह 8 बजे अकाउंट्स, बाकी बाद में। जो चैप्टर हफ्ते भर सताता था, 3 दिन में खत्म।', 'सबसे कठिन काम पहले — दिन घिसने से पहले इच्छाशक्ति सबसे ऊंची होती है।', 'कल का "मेंढक" आज रात चुनकर लिस्ट में सबसे ऊपर लिखिए।', 88, 'hi'),
  ('fin-016', 'Never mix insurance and investment', 'finance', 50, null, null, 'Why are bundled insurance-investment plans usually a bad deal?', array['They are illegal in India', 'Low cover, poor 4–5% returns, and exit penalties — worse at both jobs', 'They only cover accidental death', 'Premiums increase every year'], 1, 'The policy your uncle sold you does two jobs. It does both of them badly.', 'Endowment and money-back plans bundle insurance with investment: the cover is tiny, the returns are 4–5%, and exit penalties trap you. Unbundle: a term plan buys pure protection cheaply; the money saved compounds properly in mutual funds.', '₹30,000/year in an endowment plan buys a ₹5 lakh cover and ~5% returns. The same ₹30,000 split: a ₹1 crore term plan costs ~₹12,000 at age 25, and the remaining ₹18,000/year in an index fund at 12% builds ~₹18 lakh in 20 years — 20x the protection AND more wealth.', 'Term plan for protection, mutual funds for growth — never one product for both.', 'Check any policy you hold: if it "matures with returns", calculate its real annual return.', 91, 'en'),
  ('fin-017', 'Rent vs buy: run the 5% test', 'finance', 50, null, null, 'What does the 5% rule compare?', array['Home loan rates across banks', 'Annual rent against the invisible yearly cost of owning (~5% of price)', 'Down payment against monthly EMI', 'Property appreciation against inflation'], 1, '"Rent is money down the drain" — the most expensive sentence in Indian family WhatsApp groups.', 'Owning has invisible costs: property tax, maintenance, and the returns your down payment stops earning — roughly 5% of the home value yearly. The test: if annual rent is under 5% of the price of a similar home, renting and investing the difference often builds more wealth.', 'A ₹1 crore flat: 5% = ₹5 lakh/year (~₹42,000/month) of ownership cost. If the same flat rents for ₹25,000/month (₹3 lakh/year), renting is cheaper — and the ₹20+ lakh down payment keeps compounding in equity instead of sitting in bricks.', 'If yearly rent < 5% of the home price, renting + investing usually wins.', 'Find the price and rent of a flat you like; divide rent×12 by price.', 90, 'en'),
  ('fin-018', 'Your FIRE number: 25x annual expenses', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'Why does reducing expenses accelerate financial independence twice over?', array['It qualifies you for better credit cards', 'You save more AND the target itself (25x expenses) gets smaller', 'Banks give higher interest to frugal customers', 'It reduces income tax'], 1, '"How much money is enough to never work again?" There is an actual number. Yours.', 'The FIRE (Financial Independence, Retire Early) rule of thumb: 25 times your annual expenses, invested. At that size, withdrawing ~4% a year covers your spending while the corpus keeps growing with markets. It’s a compass, not a guarantee — India’s inflation argues for 30x.', 'Spending ₹50,000/month = ₹6 lakh/year → FIRE number ≈ ₹1.5–1.8 crore. Sounds huge, but a ₹30,000/month SIP at 12% reaches it in ~20 years. The insight: your expenses set the target — every ₹5,000 of permanent lifestyle costs adds ₹15 lakh to the number.', 'Freedom = 25–30x your annual expenses; cutting spending shrinks the goal itself.', 'Multiply your monthly spend by 300. That’s your first freedom estimate.', 92, 'en'),
  ('fin-019', 'F&O trading: the 9-out-of-10 statistic', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'Per SEBI’s study, roughly what fraction of retail F&O traders lose money?', array['1 in 10', 'Half', '7 in 10', '9 in 10'], 3, 'SEBI studied lakhs of retail F&O traders. 9 out of 10 lost money. The average loss: over ₹1 lakh.', 'Futures and options are zero-sum with leverage: your gain is someone’s loss, minus brokerage and taxes. Retail traders face institutions with faster data and algorithms. Leverage means a 5% adverse move can wipe the entire margin — it amplifies mistakes more often than wins.', 'A trader buys weekly index options with ₹20,000 — a "small punt". Options lose value every day (time decay), so even a sideways market bleeds the position. Three Fridays later the ₹20,000 is gone. Meanwhile the boring index fund holder is up for the month.', 'F&O is a professionals’ zero-sum arena — the 90% loss statistic is the product, not bad luck.', 'If you trade F&O, total your last 12 months of P&L including charges — honestly.', 93, 'en'),
  ('fin-020', 'BNPL: the friendliest-looking debt trap', 'finance', 45, null, null, 'What makes BNPL riskier than it feels?', array['It charges upfront interest on every purchase', 'Scattered small dues are easy to miss, and misses mean fees plus credit-score damage', 'It only works at select stores', 'It requires a bank guarantee'], 1, '"Pay in 3 easy installments" — the word doing all the work in that sentence is "easy".', 'Buy-Now-Pay-Later feels free but is engineered to grow your spending: it removes the pain of paying, stacks multiple small dues that are easy to lose track of, and one missed installment brings late fees plus a credit-score hit. BNPL defaults are now reported to credit bureaus.', 'Three "harmless" BNPL purchases of ₹2,000 each across apps = ₹6,000 of dues on different dates. Miss one while traveling: ₹350 late fee plus a CIBIL entry that quietly raises your future home-loan rate. The ₹2,000 sneakers now cost lakhs in extra interest.', 'BNPL is debt in a costume — if you wouldn’t swipe a credit card for it, don’t split it.', 'Count your active BNPL/pay-later dues across all apps right now.', 89, 'en'),
  ('tech-016', 'How UPI moves your money in 3 seconds', 'technology', 50, null, null, 'What role does a UPI app like GPay or PhonePe actually play?', array['It holds your money in a wallet', 'It carries signed payment instructions between banks via NPCI', 'It lends you money for each payment', 'It converts money into digital currency'], 1, 'You scan a QR, and money leaves your bank for a chaiwala’s bank in 3 seconds. Here’s the relay race.', 'UPI is a common language (protocol) between banks, run by NPCI. Your UPI app never holds money — it sends an instruction: your bank debits you, NPCI routes the message, the receiver’s bank credits them. The QR code is just the receiver’s address, and your PIN signs the instruction.', 'Paying a street vendor: the QR encodes "merchant@paytm". Your app sends the payment instruction to NPCI’s switch, which finds the vendor’s bank behind that address. Two bank ledgers update, both parties get an SMS — no cash, no card machine, no app-to-app compatibility problem.', 'UPI apps don’t hold money — they carry signed instructions between bank ledgers via NPCI.', 'Notice your next UPI payment: the receiving app can be different from yours — that’s the protocol working.', 92, 'en'),
  ('tech-017', 'What happens when you type a URL', 'technology', 55, null, null, 'What does DNS do?', array['Encrypts your connection to the website', 'Translates a domain name into a server’s IP address', 'Blocks malicious websites', 'Compresses the webpage for faster loading'], 1, 'Between pressing Enter and seeing Google, your request travels the planet. In 200 milliseconds.', 'Four steps: DNS looks up the domain’s address (like a phonebook: google.com → an IP address), your browser opens a secure connection to that server, sends the request, and the server responds with the page — which the browser then assembles from dozens of follow-up requests for images, styles, and scripts.', 'Type flipkart.com: DNS resolves it to a nearby CDN server (that’s why it’s fast in India), the TLS handshake sets up encryption, then ~50 requests fetch the page pieces. A slow site usually isn’t your internet — it’s too many round trips.', 'DNS finds the address, TLS secures the line, then dozens of requests assemble the page.', 'Open a site with your browser’s network tab visible — count the requests one page makes.', 90, 'en'),
  ('tech-018', 'Sync is not backup — and it can delete everything', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'What is the key difference between sync and backup?', array['Backup is faster than sync', 'Sync mirrors deletions too; backup keeps independent copies that survive mistakes', 'Sync works only on Wi-Fi', 'Backup requires a paid subscription'], 1, 'She deleted photos to free phone space. Google Photos "helpfully" deleted them everywhere.', 'Sync mirrors your actions across devices — including deletions. Backup keeps independent copies that survive your mistakes. Most "my photos are in the cloud" setups are sync, not backup. The gold standard is 3-2-1: three copies, two different media, one elsewhere.', 'Phone photos synced to Google Photos: delete on the phone → gone from the cloud too (recoverable only within the trash window). A true backup is the extra copy on a hard disk or a second service that nothing auto-deletes. One is convenience; the other is insurance.', 'Sync copies your mistakes; backup survives them. Keep one copy nothing can auto-delete.', 'Check whether deleting a photo on your phone removes it from your cloud service too.', 89, 'en'),
  ('tech-019', 'The 100ms auction behind every free app', 'technology', 50, null, null, 'In real-time bidding, what are advertisers actually bidding on?', array['The app’s monthly subscription revenue', 'A specific ad slot shown to a specific user profile, right now', 'The right to collect the app’s data', 'Search engine ranking positions'], 1, 'Every time a page loads, an auction for your eyeballs starts and ends before the page appears.', 'Free apps sell ad slots through real-time bidding: the moment you open a page, your anonymized profile (location, device, interests) is broadcast to advertisers, whose algorithms bid within ~100 milliseconds. Highest bidder’s ad renders. You are not the customer; your attention is the inventory.', 'A user researching flights sees hotel ads everywhere within minutes — travel advertisers bid more for someone "in market". That’s why the same news page shows you sneakers and your father loan offers: different profiles, different auctions, same slot.', '"Free" apps auction your attention in real time — the ad you see is the winning bid.', 'Notice one ad today and reverse-engineer which of your recent actions likely triggered the bid.', 90, 'en'),
  ('tech-020', 'Password managers: one lock for every door', 'technology', 45, null, null, 'Why is password reuse the biggest practical risk?', array['Reused passwords are easier to guess', 'One site’s breach lets bots log into all your other accounts within hours', 'Websites share passwords with each other', 'Browsers refuse to save reused passwords'], 1, 'You reuse one password everywhere. So one leaked site = every account breached. There’s a fix.', 'A password manager generates a unique random password per site and stores them all in an encrypted vault behind one strong master password. Reuse is the real killer — breach databases are traded and "credential stuffing" bots try your leaked combo on every major service within hours.', 'A shopping site you used in 2022 gets breached. Bots immediately try that email+password on Gmail, Paytm, and Instagram. With unique passwords, the breach ends at that one dead account. The vault also auto-fills only on the real domain — a free phishing detector.', 'One strong master password + unique generated passwords everywhere ends the reuse problem.', 'Install a password manager and migrate just your email account today — it’s the master key.', 91, 'en'),
  ('comm-016', 'Cold messages that actually get replies', 'communication', 50, null, null, 'What makes a cold message likely to get a reply?', array['Sending it to many people at once', 'Specific homework about them plus one question answerable in two minutes', 'Requesting a short call politely', 'Mentioning mutual connections in the first line'], 1, '"Hi sir, please guide me" has never once received guidance. Here’s what works instead.', 'A reply-worthy cold message has four parts: proof you did homework (reference their specific work), one precise question they can answer in two minutes, why them specifically, and zero requests for calls or "guidance". Specificity signals seriousness; vagueness signals spam.', '"Hi Meera — your talk on pricing experiments at ProductCon changed how I set up my college startup’s plans. We’re stuck between per-seat and usage pricing for a 50-user pilot. Which did you find easier to walk back from? (2-min question, promise.)" Reply rate: dramatically higher than "please guide me".', 'Homework + one 2-minute question + why them = replies. Never ask strangers for calls.', 'Rewrite one pending cold message to contain exactly one specific question.', 91, 'en'),
  ('comm-017', 'Own the notes, own the narrative', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'Why is writing the meeting summary a position of influence?', array['It excuses you from action items', 'The written record becomes the accepted version of what was agreed', 'Managers promote note-takers faster', 'It shortens future meetings'], 1, 'The least glamorous job in every meeting is secretly the most powerful one.', 'The person who writes the summary decides what was agreed. Volunteering for notes gives you: everyone reads your words, disputes get resolved by YOUR record, and action items carry the framing you gave them. Send within an hour: decisions, owners, deadlines — three sections, bullets only.', 'After a chaotic project call, Priya sends: "Decisions: launch moves to March 3. Owners: design QA — Rahul by Fri. Open: budget for reshoot — Anil to confirm Monday." A week later, when memories blur, her email IS the meeting. Quietly, she’s become the project’s reference point.', 'Volunteer to send the summary — the written record outlives the meeting.', 'In your next meeting, send the recap within an hour: decisions, owners, deadlines.', 88, 'en'),
  ('comm-018', 'Apologize like a professional', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'What ruins an otherwise good apology?', array['Making it in writing instead of in person', 'Attaching a "but" and leading with explanations before ownership', 'Keeping it under four sentences', 'Proposing a fix too quickly'], 1, '"Sorry you feel that way" is not an apology. It’s an accusation wearing an apology’s clothes.', 'A professional apology has four parts and no "but": name what you did specifically, acknowledge the impact on them, state the fix and what changes next time, then stop talking. Explanations before ownership sound like excuses; the order matters more than the words.', 'Instead of "Sorry, but the client kept changing scope": "I missed Thursday’s deadline, which forced you to present without the numbers. The deck is done now, and I’ve set a mid-week checkpoint so you’ll know early if anything slips again." Trust often ends up higher than before the mistake.', 'Name it, own the impact, state the fix — and never attach a "but".', 'If you owe anyone a real apology, send the four-part version today.', 90, 'en'),
  ('comm-019', 'Pauses beat vocabulary', 'communication', 40, null, null, 'What are filler words, functionally?', array['Signs of a weak vocabulary', 'Pauses the speaker was afraid to take silently', 'A habit only non-native speakers have', 'Necessary connectors between ideas'], 1, 'The most confident thing you can do mid-sentence is stop talking. On purpose.', 'Fillers ("umm", "like", "basically") leak anxiety; pauses project control. A deliberate one-second pause before answering signals thought, lands your key point, and kills fillers — because fillers are just pauses you were afraid to take. Slow speakers are consistently rated more credible.', 'In placement interviews, two students give the same answer. One rushes, umm-ing through it. The other pauses a beat, then speaks 20% slower with a full stop after the key claim. Interviewers describe the second as "composed and senior" — identical content, different tempo.', 'Replace fillers with silence — a deliberate pause reads as confidence, not confusion.', 'In your next conversation, take a one-second pause before every answer.', 89, 'en'),
  ('comm-020', 'Networking for introverts: depth beats breadth', 'communication', 45, null, null, 'What is the introvert-friendly core of effective networking?', array['Attending more events to build tolerance', 'Few deep conversations, specific follow-ups, and giving before asking', 'Preparing a memorized elevator pitch', 'Connecting with everyone on LinkedIn immediately'], 1, 'You dread "networking events". Good news: collecting 30 business cards was never the point.', 'Useful networks are built on depth: two real conversations beat twenty introductions, because people help those they remember. The introvert’s edge is listening — ask about their current problem, follow up within 48 hours referencing specifics, and offer something small before asking anything.', 'At a college fest, Arjun skips the crowd and has one long chat with a speaker about her hiring struggles. Next day he emails two portfolio sites of juniors who fit. Six months later, she calls HIM about an internship. One conversation, remembered; thirty handshakes, forgotten.', 'Two deep conversations + a specific 48-hour follow-up beat a stack of introductions.', 'Follow up with one person you met recently, referencing something specific they said.', 88, 'en'),
  ('prod-016', 'If-then plans: decide once, act automatically', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'Why do if-then plans outperform plain goals?', array['They are more ambitious', 'The decision is made in advance, so the moment of choice needs no willpower', 'They involve accountability partners', 'They include rewards for completion'], 1, 'People who plan WHEN and WHERE they’ll act are 2–3x more likely to actually do it.', 'An implementation intention pre-loads a decision: "If [situation X], then I will [action Y]." Goals fail at the moment of choice — the if-then removes the choice by deciding in advance. The situation becomes the trigger, so willpower never enters the picture.', '"I’ll exercise more" fails. "If it’s 7am on Mon/Wed/Fri, then I put on shoes and walk 20 minutes" survives. Also works for derailments: "If I open Instagram during study hours, then I close it and do 10 minutes of SkillScroll first." The plan fires before the debate starts.', 'Don’t set goals — set triggers: "If X happens, then I do Y."', 'Convert one vague goal into an if-then sentence and put it where the "if" happens.', 91, 'en'),
  ('prod-017', 'Open loops are eating your focus', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'Per the Zeigarnik effect, how do you quiet an unfinished task you can’t do now?', array['Ignore it until it stops resurfacing', 'Write it down with a specific time and place to handle it', 'Do it immediately regardless of priorities', 'Delegate it to someone else'], 1, 'That unsent reply you keep remembering at random moments? Your brain is running it as a background app.', 'The Zeigarnik effect: unfinished tasks keep intruding on your thoughts — the brain rehearses open loops so you won’t forget them. The fix isn’t finishing everything; it’s capturing everything. Writing a task down with a when-and-where releases the mental loop almost as well as doing it.', 'Mid-study, Arjun keeps remembering the fest sponsorship email, a form deadline, and mom’s recharge. Each intrusion costs minutes of refocus. He keeps a capture pad: 10 seconds to write "recharge — tonight 9pm", and the thought stops resurfacing. His study block finally holds.', 'You can’t close every loop today — but capturing it with a "when" silences it.', 'Do a 5-minute brain dump of every open loop, each with a scheduled when.', 90, 'en'),
  ('prod-018', 'Temptation bundling: bribe yourself, scientifically', 'productivity', 40, null, null, 'What is the rule that makes temptation bundling work?', array['Reward yourself after finishing the task', 'The pleasure is permitted only during the avoided activity, never separately', 'Alternate one fun day with one productive day', 'Remove all temptations during work hours'], 1, 'What if the show you binge could only be watched while doing the thing you avoid?', 'Temptation bundling pairs a want with a should: the guilty pleasure is allowed ONLY during the avoided activity. The temptation becomes the fuel instead of the distraction — you start looking forward to the chore because it’s the only doorway to the treat.', 'Podcast episodes only while at the gym. That web series only while ironing or on the commute-study flashcards. Priya lets herself order her favorite coffee only when she’s doing weekly expense tracking at the café — a chore she skipped for months now happens like clockwork.', 'Chain the guilty pleasure to the avoided task — want fuels should.', 'Pick one indulgence and one avoided task; allow the first only during the second.', 88, 'en'),
  ('prod-019', 'Study the 20% that carries 80% of marks', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'What should you do BEFORE deep studying, per the 80/20 approach?', array['Read the full textbook once for overview', 'Analyze past papers/weightage to find the high-yield core', 'Make a colorful timetable', 'Join a study group'], 1, 'Toppers don’t study everything. They study the right things twice.', 'The Pareto principle in learning: a minority of concepts carries most of the outcomes — exam marks, interview answers, real-world use. Before grinding, invest an hour finding the high-yield core: past papers, syllabus weightage, and what practitioners actually use daily.', 'Five years of past papers reveal that 4 of 12 chapters produce 70% of questions. Arjun masters those four with problems and revision, then skims the rest. Same hours as his "cover everything once" friends — very different scoreline. The analysis hour was the highest-yield hour.', 'Find the high-yield 20% first — mastery of the core beats coverage of the whole.', 'For your current subject, scan past papers and list the top 3 recurring topics.', 90, 'en'),
  ('prod-020', 'Design your defaults: make good choices lazy', 'productivity', 45, null, null, 'What is the core mechanism of environment design?', array['Increasing motivation through visual reminders', 'Adjusting friction so the easiest path is the desired behavior', 'Scheduling every hour of the day', 'Working only in libraries or cafés'], 1, 'You don’t rise to your discipline. You fall to your environment. So rig the environment.', 'Behavior follows friction: we do what’s easy and skip what takes extra steps. Environment design means adding steps to bad defaults (log out, uninstall, phone in another room) and removing steps from good ones (book open on the desk, shoes by the door, app on the home screen).', 'Rahul moved Instagram into a folder on screen 3, logged out, and put SkillScroll where it used to be. Phone checks still happen — but now the lazy path lands on a lesson. He didn’t become more disciplined; unlocking-scrolling-Instagram just stopped being the path of least resistance.', 'Add friction to bad defaults, remove it from good ones — laziness becomes your ally.', 'Move one distracting app off your home screen and one useful app onto it.', 91, 'en')
